<section image-layout="blank" id="B-uYnOS4oX" vertical-align="center" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.38"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Mg_38a9d0yi7UoPDDzimc.png" saved-media-id="yp22nbuc3imcvwu" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of a brass telescope aimed across a vast deep-space field, precise ivory coordinate arcs widening across an abyssal-blue sky, cyan spectral traces and one restrained coral signal, a tiny generic ship silhouette low at the far right for scale, generous dark negative space on the left, no central explosion, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="LPXLeEPBIr"><p role="eyebrow">No shore, no center</p><h1 font-size="52pt" style="padding-right: 34%;">Captain, reverse the telescope</h1><p font-size="lg" style="padding-right: 42%;"><b>Across distant galaxies, one clue repeats:</b> the universe is not debris racing from a blast point. Space itself has been stretching for about 13.8 billion years.</p><spacer size="24pt"></spacer><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="xs"><p font-size="sm">Our course follows three bearings · Expansion · Cooling · Evidence</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="54Yssyw4l1"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="Vp3q-YxvKg" vertical-align="center" colspan="34"><labels><label variant="outline">Log entry 01</label></labels><h2>The log begins hot and dense</h2><p>Open the log with the observable universe in an extremely hot, dense early state. As space expanded, the universe cooled. Measurements place this history at about <b>13.8 billion years</b>.</p><p>This is not a bomb’s launch time in a pre-existing void. The model describes the universe changing with time, not matter flying into an outer sea.</p><p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/">NASA · Universe Overview</a></p></grid-item><grid-item id="926XgcszlN" padding="0" colspan="26"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/6BUX-X3EdQCLCcUmJ_1Kf.png" saved-media-id="l62694v2qznc0uy" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of the early cosmos as a uniformly dense luminous field extending beyond the frame, nested ivory coordinate meshes, oxidized-brass scientific instrument arcs, cyan radiation traces diffused everywhere with one restrained coral highlight, no central point, no outward blast, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="G7QUWdMXzz"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="jLYnoNS_fy"><h2>Stretch the chart, not the ships</h2><p><b>Chart test:</b> mark three galaxies on a flexible grid, then enlarge the grid. Every pair separates; from any marker, the other two recede. No marker becomes the privileged center.</p><freeform-block w="840" h="230"><shape preset="roundRect" y="8" w="360" h="206" fill="surface" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="roundRect" x="480" y="8" w="360" h="206" fill="surface" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="70" y="38" w="0" h="150" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="145" y="38" w="0" h="150" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="220" y="38" w="0" h="150" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="295" y="38" w="0" h="150" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="38" y="65" w="284" h="0" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="38" y="112" w="284" h="0" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="38" y="159" w="284" h="0" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="100" y="76" w="150" h="10" stroke="accent" stroke-width="2"></shape><shape preset="line" x="175" y="86" w="75" h="84" stroke="accent" stroke-width="2" flip-h="true"></shape><shape preset="line" x="100" y="76" w="75" h="94" stroke="accent" stroke-width="2"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="92" y="68" w="16" h="16" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="242" y="78" w="16" h="16" fill="palette-3" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="167" y="162" w="16" h="16" fill="accent" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="rightArrow" x="397" y="96" w="46" h="28" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="line" x="540" y="28" w="0" h="174" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="620" y="28" w="0" h="174" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="700" y="28" w="0" h="174" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="780" y="28" w="0" h="174" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="500" y="52" w="320" h="0" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="500" y="112" w="320" h="0" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="500" y="172" w="320" h="0" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="line" x="520" y="48" w="270" h="10" stroke="accent" stroke-width="2"></shape><shape preset="line" x="650" y="58" w="140" h="130" stroke="accent" stroke-width="2" flip-h="true"></shape><shape preset="line" x="520" y="48" w="130" h="140" stroke="accent" stroke-width="2"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="512" y="40" w="16" h="16" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="782" y="50" w="16" h="16" fill="palette-3" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="642" y="180" w="16" h="16" fill="accent" stroke-width="0"></shape><box type="content" x="18" y="16" w="145"><p font-size="sm"><b>Same grid markers</b></p></box><box type="content" x="498" y="16" w="190"><p font-size="sm"><b>Grid enlarged</b></p></box><box type="content" x="80" y="82" w="24"><p font-size="sm">A</p></box><box type="content" x="260" y="80" w="24"><p font-size="sm">B</p></box><box type="content" x="184" y="170" w="24"><p font-size="sm">C</p></box><box type="content" x="500" y="58" w="24"><p font-size="sm">A</p></box><box type="content" x="802" y="50" w="24"><p font-size="sm">B</p></box><box type="content" x="660" y="181" w="24"><p font-size="sm">C</p></box></freeform-block><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="XQGBp8BldJ" colspan="30"><p font-size="sm"><b>The grid</b> represents changing distances in space.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="BmtdeV67EM" colspan="30"><p font-size="sm"><b>The markers</b> are not ships crossing a surrounding cosmic ocean.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="OQY-X6x27W"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="9EmSBbsNu8"><h2>Expansion cools the cosmos</h2><p><b>As the universe expands, it cools—and what matter and light can do changes.</b> Follow the sequence in the log; each stage depends on the conditions before it.</p><smart-layout variant="minimalTimeline" orientation="horizontal" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>Expansion and cooling</h4><p>Growing distances accompany falling temperature.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Early · Nuclei form</h4><p>Atomic nuclei can persist as conditions cool.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>About 380,000 years</h4><p>Neutral atoms form; light can travel freely.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Later · Structure emerges</h4><p>Stars and galaxies develop over cosmic time.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="Gppcu1tgFB"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="B5whB3WR2b"><h2>Redshift: light keeps the wake</h2><p>During a galaxy’s light voyage, expanding space stretches the light’s wavelength toward longer, redder bands. This <b>cosmological redshift</b> is a stretched signal—not dye, smoke, or a red sail.</p><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="0PtIiZ-dCJ" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">At emission</p><h3>Shorter wavelength</h3><custom-code>
            <style>
              .wake-short { height: 7.2em; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; }
              .wake-short svg { max-width: 100%; }
            </style>
            <div class="wake-short">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 360 105" width="360" height="105" role="img" aria-label="A tightly spaced light wave">
                <line x1="10" y1="53" x2="350" y2="53" stroke="var(--body-color)" stroke-opacity=".25" stroke-dasharray="5 6"></line>
                <path d="M10 53 C20 17 30 17 40 53 S60 89 70 53 S90 17 100 53 S120 89 130 53 S150 17 160 53 S180 89 190 53 S210 17 220 53 S240 89 250 53 S270 17 280 53 S300 89 310 53 S330 17 350 53" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent-color)" stroke-width="4"></path>
                <circle cx="10" cy="53" r="6" fill="var(--warm-color, var(--accent-color))"></circle>
                <circle cx="350" cy="53" r="6" fill="var(--accent-color)"></circle>
              </svg>
            </div>
          </custom-code><p font-size="sm">Successive crests begin close together.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="FpUkipYB8f" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">After cosmic travel</p><h3>Longer wavelength</h3><custom-code>
            <style>
              .wake-long { height: 7.2em; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; }
              .wake-long svg { max-width: 100%; }
            </style>
            <div class="wake-long">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 360 105" width="360" height="105" role="img" aria-label="A stretched light wave with wider spacing">
                <line x1="10" y1="53" x2="350" y2="53" stroke="var(--body-color)" stroke-opacity=".25" stroke-dasharray="5 6"></line>
                <path d="M10 53 C38 12 66 12 94 53 S150 94 178 53 S234 12 262 53 S318 94 350 53" fill="none" stroke="var(--palette-3, var(--accent-color))" stroke-width="4"></path>
                <circle cx="10" cy="53" r="6" fill="var(--accent-color)"></circle>
                <circle cx="350" cy="53" r="6" fill="var(--palette-3, var(--accent-color))"></circle>
              </svg>
            </div>
          </custom-code><p font-size="sm">Expansion increases the crest-to-crest distance.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="sm">Across distant galaxies, this pattern is evidence that space expanded while their light traveled. Source: <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-behind-the-discoveries/hubble-big-bang/">NASA/Hubble · Science Behind the Big Bang</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="dgwqTqMjen"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="KjI3GeAm2K" vertical-align="center" colspan="25"><p role="eyebrow">Evidence bearing 02</p><h2>Relic light on every horizon</h2><display size="md" font-size="48pt">≈380,000 years</display><p>After the hot beginning, the universe became transparent enough for light to travel freely. Expansion has since cooled that light into microwaves.</p><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="xs"><h4>Cosmic microwave background</h4><p>Relic light from the early universe—seen across the sky, not pointing back to one central blast port.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="5_L16S4Eom" padding="0" colspan="35"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/LMoZ5ce4iSpYq4ackgd_C.png" saved-media-id="orxne0y5h5i7x1r" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of relic microwave light arriving from every horizon, an observer's scientific instrument at the lower edge surrounded by a continuous sphere of faint mottled radiation, abyssal-blue field, crisp ivory coordinate arcs, oxidized-brass fittings, cyan glow with sparse coral signal flecks, no center point, no explosion, no chart labels or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="rgYe7pz2V3" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="c5ebnafYzH"><h2>A good chart marks its limits</h2><p><b>The model charts evolution, not every origin question.</b> Honest navigators distinguish what the evidence-supported course describes from what remains beyond the chart.</p><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="xStgClGF_n" background-color="surface" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="solid">Within the chart</label></labels><h3>Development of the observable universe</h3><p>A hot, dense early phase followed by expansion and cooling. The account includes the formation of nuclei, atoms, freely traveling light, stars, and galaxies.</p><p><b>No privileged center or navigable edge is required.</b></p></grid-item><grid-item id="ADjs_arDBY" cell-style="outline" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="outline">Boundary marked</label></labels><h3>Before the earliest described phase</h3><p>What preceded that phase remains uncertain. The Big Bang model does not turn an open question into a known launch point, outer void, or certain first cause.</p><p><b>The limit is part of an honest chart.</b></p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="-ZBEtibu6V" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="XAR-suzOL4"><p role="eyebrow">Final position</p><h2>Three bearings bring us home</h2><p><b>Keep these bearings together.</b> Each describes the same cosmic history from a different angle.</p><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-straddle="true" spacing="medium" padding-top="42pt"><grid-item id="9r7zYoaDQS" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/liSFBNn5c6cmyoTD4ajN.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="expanding coordinate grid" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A grid of intersecting horizontal and vertical lines, forming a pattern of squares, symbolizing organization, structure, and layout.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Expansion</h3><p>Space stretches; distances grow. The change is not motion away from one cosmic port.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="D-EoLe__H2" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/ftaKq9VUqfQkLeYWRZCa.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="cooling temperature instrument" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Since there is no actual image provided, I will assume a common symbol for temperature. \n\nA thermometer icon, often depicted as a simple black shape with a bulb at the bottom and a rising line, situated within a white background, embodies the concept of temperature. This visual representation is widely recognized and utilized across various platforms to signify temperature readings, measurements, or adjustments. The straightforward design allows for easy identification and understanding of its relation to temperature.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Cooling</h3><p>Changing conditions allow nuclei, atoms, stars, and galaxies to form in sequence.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="JD6Wif6Xdq" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/haeiKVXiokADxeUKtjLaRm.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="spectral wave and microwave signal" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;The wave-square concept features a solid black square with a curved wave-like cutout or indentation on one of its sides, set against a clean white background. This design embodies the idea of a wave pattern integrated into a geometric square shape, suggesting a fusion of fluidity and structure. The wave element could symbolize movement, oscillation, or the dynamic nature of certain phenomena, while the square provides a sense of stability and formality. Together, they create a visually striking and thought-provoking icon that could represent concepts related to signal processing, sound waves, or the blending of natural and geometric forms.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h3>Evidence</h3><p>Cosmological redshift and relic microwave light fit that same expanding history.</p></grid-item></grid><spacer size="18pt"></spacer><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="85X9VyONww" background-color="accent" padding="14pt 18pt"><p font-size="lg"><b>If asked where it happened, answer:</b> not at one cosmic port—the change belongs to the universe itself.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>